Your house analogy is genuinely good, and most of it maps correctly — but there's one specific detail worth correcting, because it's an important one.

What you got right:

The house = your whole application/website.
Being a "child of the owner" = having a real, existing account (you registered, you got verified) — this is your identity.
The key = the login token — proof you're allowed in, obtained through the login process.
TV, water, bed, kitchen access = regular authenticated features — anyone with a valid key can use these (this is authentication — "are you a real, logged-in member of this house?").
Climbing the chair, only for the house owner = admin-only permissions — this is authorization — a second, stricter check layered on top of just having a key.
That's a genuinely accurate way to think about authentication vs authorization — nicely done.

The one correction: it's not that "once you're inside, you can move anywhere freely without further checks."

Here's the more accurate version: every single room in the house has its own lock on its door — the kitchen has a lock, the bedroom has a lock, the living room has a lock. Your key might open all of them (if you're a regular authenticated member), but you still have to use your key at each door, every single time you want to enter that room. You don't just get "checked once at the front door" and then wander freely with no further checks — each room independently verifies you have a valid key before letting you in.

This maps directly to auth middleware: it's not something that runs once and then "remembers" you're allowed everywhere afterward. It runs again, independently, on every single request to every protected route:

javascript
router.get('/homepage', auth, getHomepage);    // checks your key AGAIN, here
router.get('/marketplace', auth, getMarketplace); // checks your key AGAIN, here
router.post('/logout', auth, logout);          // checks your key AGAIN, here
Each of these routes independently runs auth — none of them "trust" that you were already checked somewhere else. Your key (token) is simply presented again each time, and each door (route) checks it fresh, every time.

Where "climbing the chair" fits in, using the same idea: the chair-climbing room doesn't just have a regular lock — it has two locks stacked on the same door: your regular key opens the first lock (proving you're a real member of the house — auth), but there's a second lock that only the house owner's special key opens (requireAdmin). You need to pass through both locks to get into that specific room; everyone else's key stops working at the second lock.

So the one thing to adjust in your mental model: it's not "checked once, then free to roam." It's "checked fresh, every single time, at every single door" — with certain special doors having an extra second lock on top.

